# No Em Dashes

Do not use em dashes (`—`, U+2014) in any output. This includes assistant
messages, code comments, docs, commit messages, PR descriptions, generated
prose, and anything else.

The rule applies regardless of context. When summarizing or paraphrasing
other text, substitute or rephrase rather than copying an em dash through.

## Substitutes (pick whichever fits)

- **Period.** Best default. Splits the thought cleanly.
- **Comma,** when the second clause modifies the first.
- **Colon:** when the second clause defines, lists, or expands.
- **Parentheses** for true asides.
- **" - "** (space hyphen space) when none of the above flows naturally.

## Examples

BAD
- The build is green — all tests passed.
- This took longer than expected — it was the first migration of its kind.
- Three fixes are open — auth, retries, and logging.

GOOD
- The build is green. All tests passed.
- This took longer than expected because it was the first migration of its kind.
- Three fixes are open: auth, retries, and logging.

## Other dashes are fine

- **Hyphen** (`-`, in compound words like `well-known`, `read-only`): keep as is.
- **En dash** (`–`, U+2013, in numeric ranges like `May 19–20`): keep as is.
- **Em dash** (`—`, U+2014): do not use.

If you can't tell whether a character is an em dash or an en dash at a glance,
default to not using it and rephrase the sentence.
